is issue we create the peering  again and use the correct tenant project  on gcpe site  great now it is showing as connected  let's run the pin command again  it's still not working this could likely  be due to a routing issue  to troubleshoot this identify the IP  address of the source compute engine VM  that you were trying to connect from by  navigating to the compute engine page in  the Google Cloud console  the IP address is 10.128.0.3  connect to the gcpe portal and in the  private connection tab check the  imported routes check if the GCE VM IP  address exists in the range that are  imported  yes the range is here  check the exported routes oh here you go  the routes are showing rejected this is  the reason why the VM is unable to reach  the zcve resources  now let's verify the same in the Google  Cloud console site go back to the  peering connection again and check the  imported route you should be able to see  the subnet range advertised oh here we  see the routes showing status rejected  by configuration this means that the  Import and Export custom routes are not  enabled  to fix this issue click on edit and  check the options Import and Export  custom routes save the changes let's  check the imported and exported routes  again yay it is showing as accepted all  right let's run the pen command again  phew the GCV VM is now reachable  if you are still facing an issue after  checking these options for custom routes  then follow these steps connect to the  compute engine VM instance and run the  trace route command to the vcenter or  any gcve workload machine  review the output and verify if the  correct route is used to gcve machine  it is not showing anything to  investigate this further in the Google  Cloud console go to VPC Tab and select  routes check that out to gcpe workloads  ah this is the culprit here this route  has an incorrect Target IP let's delete  this route  test it again  it is now working we are able to connect  from Google Cloud VM to gcve finally if  you are unable to connect to gcve from  on-prem then it could likely be because  the routes may not have been advertised  from your on-prem to your VPC to verify  if the routes are advertised go to the  VPC page in Google Cloud console check  the imported routes  similarly you can check the imported  routes in the private connection tab in  gcve console if the routes are  advertised then you will see them here  if not you need to advertise them in  your on-prem setup to fix the issue for  more information on connecting from your  Google Cloud to your GCE resources check  out this documentation  thanks for watching  [Music]

